## The three bugs

<CauseTable
  ticket={4471}
  filed={{
    parser: { label: 'mega_dev#1', href: 'https://github.com/krzysztofgrys/mega_dev/issues/1' },
    hint: { label: 'mega_dev#2', href: 'https://github.com/krzysztofgrys/mega_dev/issues/2' },
  }}
/>

| cause | what happens |
| --- | --- |
| `parser` | The shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants get day and month swapped in app, email and the booking-v2 form. |
| `hint` | The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. People type the month first, the form reads the day first. Only through the form, only on booking-v3. |
| one-day move | The date lands one day off, with no swap. Looks like a time zone problem; not verified. Filed by tenant under `parser` above, footnoted under it. Ticket: [mega_dev#3](https://github.com/krzysztofgrys/mega_dev/issues/3). |

## How the reports were split

- One id, one report: the export repeats some reports across pages. Counts use unique ids.
- The cause comes from the tenant (its locale and booking form, `locales.ts`) and the channel,
  not from the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser and is often wrong.
- The one-day moves are the reports the desk itself noted in `desk-notes.json`. The desk files
  them by tenant, so they stay in the `parser` count and show in the footnote. They are a third
  bug all the same: the parser fix does not close them.

## Lessons

- A matching subject line is not the same bug. Read the cause, then merge.
- A footnote under a count is a sign the count is wrong.

